NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows data which has been sampled at the outputs of an
A to D converter in a digital storage oscilloscope. The oscilloscope was 
sampling at 100MHz. The LogicPort synchronously sampled the data in 
state mode, yielding a resolution of 10ns.

This example shows that state mode is useful for viewing synchronous 
data values as the system under test sees them. The waveform shown 
by the LogicPort is identical to the waveform displayed by the digital 
storage oscilloscope during this acquisition.

Note that "ADC Clock" is always low in this example because it is sampling
itself on its own falling edge. If "Sample data 2.5ns before the" falling edge
was selected in the Sample Mode Setup dialogue, "ADC Clock" would always 
be high because the actual sample would be captured before the falling edge.

This example takes advantage of a couple of the LogicPort's advanced
trigger features. Notice that the trigger scheme is setup so that a trigger
occurs when the ADC's output value becomes greater then 80h after it's 
been less than 80h for more than 100ns. This has the effect of triggering 
on the "rising edge" of the analog waveform being sampled by the ADC.
 
Note that the LogicPort is able to accurately represent the period of this
500KHz sine wave (Interval A->B) because the frequency of "ADC Clock" 
has been specified in the sample setup dialogue. This also allows trigger 
duration values to be specified in Seconds. You can enter duration values 
in Samples as well. This is useful when the state clock frequency is 
unknown, or is not constant. 

The "Show Signal Column Only" option has been selected in this example
so that as much data as possible can be displayed. Turn this option on or
off by right-clicking the "Signal" cell of the waveform table.

Timing mode is more useful when we want to see the timing relationships
between different signals. To see this same data sampled in timing mode, 
open the file "ADC Sine, Timing mode 500MHz.LPF". If you compare the 
two files, you can see that state mode has made much better use of sample 
memory. This is usually the case. Because timing mode sampling is not 
synchronized to the circuit under test, a logic analyzer must sample at a 
higher rate (using more memory) to guarantee that the data can be 
accurately reconstructed.

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain 
intact for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning 
purposes, but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the 
"Save As" option to save an editable copy of any example file.
/
